{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 6159 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "d1c782011d6afc677834b2b82f6467dd31b4a5798f05d6753a9cc9a6660b0af0" :edb/db-before-t 6158 :edb/db-after-t 6159 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539471 50 #inst "2026-10-24T02:21:14.163Z" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186558525 1074 "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=110$ is the initial amount of caffeine in the adult's body.\n\n- $r=0.15$ is the decay rate ($15%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =110\\cdot (1-0.15)^{t} \\\\  & =110\\cdot (0.85)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for $t=11$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(11)=110\\cdot (0.85)^{11}≈18.\n$$\n\nHence, after $11$ hours, there will be $18$ mg of caffeine left in their system." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186558525 1074 "The formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1-r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=110$ is the initial amount of caffeine in the adult's body.\n\n- $r=0.15$ is the decay rate ($15\\%$ expressed as a decimal).\n\n- $t=$ the time, in hours.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $y$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =110\\cdot (1-0.15)^{t} \\\\  & =110\\cdot (0.85)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the expression for $t=11$ hours, we get\n\n$$\ny(11)=110\\cdot (0.85)^{11}≈18.\n$$\n\nHence, after $11$ hours, there will be $18$ mg of caffeine left in their system." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186558539 1071 "Maya and Leon get paid for painting houses. The ratio of Maya's reward to Leon's reward is $3:4.$ \nDuring a particular week, Leon received $$806.$ By rounding Leon's earnings to the nearest hundred, approximate the amount of money that Maya received." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186558539 1071 "Maya and Leon get paid for painting houses. The ratio of Maya's reward to Leon's reward is $3:4.$ \nDuring a particular week, Leon received $\\$806.$ By rounding Leon's earnings to the nearest hundred, approximate the amount of money that Maya received." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186558539 1074 "First, let's approximate the amount of money that Leon received:\n\n$$\n$806⟶$800\n$$\n\nThe ratio of Maya's earnings to Leon's earnings is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents Maya's earnings  and the denominator represents Leon's earnings.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $800$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $200$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 200}{4\\times 200}=\\frac{600}{800}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Maya received approximately $$600.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186558539 1074 "First, let's approximate the amount of money that Leon received:\n\n$$\n$806⟶$800\n$$\n\nThe ratio of Maya's earnings to Leon's earnings is\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nThe numerator represents Maya's earnings  and the denominator represents Leon's earnings.\n\nWe can make a denominator of $800$ by multiplying the numerator and denominator by \n $200$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3\\times 200}{4\\times 200}=\\frac{600}{800}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, Maya received approximately $\\$600.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186558562 1071 "A car rental agency charges a fixed fee of $$25$ plus $$0.08$ per mile for the rental of a particular car.\n\nWhat expression gives the total cost, in dollars, if the car is driven for  $m$ miles?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186558562 1071 "A car rental agency charges a fixed fee of $\\$25$ plus $\\$0.08$ per mile for the rental of a particular car.\n\nWhat expression gives the total cost, in dollars, if the car is driven for  $m$ miles?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186558562 1074 "If $m$ is the number of miles that the car is driven, and the car rental agency charges a fee of $$0.08$ per mile, then the cost for $m$ miles must be $0.08m.$ \n\nThere is also a fixed fee of $$25.$ Consequently, the constant term of the expression should be $25.$\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost (in dollars) when the car is driven for  $m$ miles must be \n\n$$\n0.08m+25\n$$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186558562 1074 "If $m$ is the number of miles that the car is driven, and the car rental agency charges a fee of $\\$0.08$ per mile, then the cost for $m$ miles must be $0.08m.$ \n\nThere is also a fixed fee of $\\$25.$ Consequently, the constant term of the expression should be $25.$\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost (in dollars) when the car is driven for  $m$ miles must be \n\n$$\n0.08m+25\n$$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186559826 1071 "![](images/d6/d67ba70d92807f0a7298fb8222252a047f7eec03a26878a5aa95b0fb82a7335c.png)\n\nA box with no lid whose depth $z$ is $3$ times its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$2$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $972\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186559826 1071 "![](images/d6/d67ba70d92807f0a7298fb8222252a047f7eec03a26878a5aa95b0fb82a7335c.png)\n\nA box with no lid whose depth $z$ is $3$ times its height $y$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $\\$2$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $\\$1$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $972\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost? Do not round the answer.\n\n$z=$\n{{field-1}} units" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186559827 1071 "![](images/af/afc68dbb5c336cff31e75f5ed92dd853c8fc7ec43e8fa66539d27d83b9e2070f.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $$3$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$5$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $2\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186559827 1071 "![](images/af/afc68dbb5c336cff31e75f5ed92dd853c8fc7ec43e8fa66539d27d83b9e2070f.png)\n\nA box with no lid and a square base has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the base costs $\\$3$ per square meter, and the material for the lateral faces costs $\\$5$ per square meter. The box must have a volume of $2\\,{m}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $x$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186559890 1071 "A  recording studio charges musicians an initial fee of $$80$ to record an album. The studio also charges an additional $$50$ per hour.\n\nWhat expression gives the cost, in dollars, of recording an album,  in terms of the number of hours $h$ used?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186559890 1071 "A  recording studio charges musicians an initial fee of $\\$80$ to record an album. The studio also charges an additional $\\$50$ per hour.\n\nWhat expression gives the cost, in dollars, of recording an album,  in terms of the number of hours $h$ used?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186559890 1074 "The fixed fee for recording an album is $$80.$ Hence,  $80$ should be a constant term.\n\nNow, if $h$ is the number of hours used to record an album, the cost  for $h$ hours of a recording must be $50h$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost, in dollars, of recording an album will be $50h+80.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186559890 1074 "The fixed fee for recording an album is $\\$80.$ Hence,  $80$ should be a constant term.\n\nNow, if $h$ is the number of hours used to record an album, the cost  for $h$ hours of a recording must be $50h$ dollars.\n\nTherefore, the expression that represents the total cost, in dollars, of recording an album will be $50h+80.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186562391 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times \\,45%=18$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186562391 1071 "What is the missing number?\n\n{{field-1}}\n$\\times \\,45\\%=18$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186562391 1074 "The part is $18,$ the percentage is $45%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $45%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =18÷45% \\\\  & =18÷\\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =18\\times \\frac{20}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{9}\\times 20 \\\\  & =2\\times 20 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $40.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186562391 1074 "The part is $18,$ the percentage is $45\\%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $45\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{45}{100}=\\frac{45÷5}{100÷5}=\\frac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =18÷45\\% \\\\  & =18÷\\frac{9}{20} \\\\  & =18\\times \\frac{20}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{9}\\times 20 \\\\  & =2\\times 20 \\\\  & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $40.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186562474 1071 "A startup launched its website with $25$ initial visitors. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after launch, the number of visitors increased by $300%$ of the number of visitors at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many visitors are there estimated to be after $3$ days?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186562474 1071 "A startup launched its website with $25$ initial visitors. An exponential model estimates that at the end of each day after launch, the number of visitors increased by $300\\%$ of the number of visitors at the end of the previous day.\n\nAccording to this model, how many visitors are there estimated to be after $3$ days?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186562474 1074 "The number of visitors $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $300%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=25$ is the total number of visitors initially\n\n- $r=3.0$ is the growth rate ($300%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=25\\cdot (1+3.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=25\\cdot (4)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of visitors after $3$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=25\\cdot (4)^{3}=25\\cdot 64=1600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of visitors after $3$ days is $1600.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186562474 1074 "The number of visitors $V(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $300\\%.$\n\nLet's write down what we know:\n\n- $a=25$ is the total number of visitors initially\n\n- $r=3.0$ is the growth rate ($300\\%$ expressed as a decimal)\n\n- $t=$ the time (in days) after launch\n\nThe formula for exponential growth is\n\n$$\nV=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $V(t)$ is\n\n$$\nV=25\\cdot (1+3.0)^{t}.\n$$\n\nSimplifying the above, we get\n\n$$\nV(t)=25\\cdot (4)^{t}.\n$$\n\nTo find the total number of visitors after $3$ days, we evaluate the above for $t=3:$\n\n$$\nV(3)=25\\cdot (4)^{3}=25\\cdot 64=1600\n$$\n\nTherefore, the estimated number of visitors after $3$ days is $1600.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186563688 1074 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-6{x}^{2}+360x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=24$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(24) & =-6(24)^{2}+360(24) \\\\  & =-3\\,456+8\\,640 \\\\  & =5\\,184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $$5\\,184.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186563688 1074 "From part 3, we have the function $p(x)=-6{x}^{2}+360x.$\n\nWe substitute $x=24$ into the function definition and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(24) & =-6(24)^{2}+360(24) \\\\  & =-3\\,456+8\\,640 \\\\  & =5\\,184\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the company will earn $\\$5\\,184.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186563759 1074 "From part 5, the required number of wooden tables $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cdot ({x}^{2}-60x+800)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $6,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-60x+800=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(800)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-60).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-40).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-60x=-20x-40x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-60x+800 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-40x+800) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-40(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-20)(x-40)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=40.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ wooden tables per week to make a profit of $$4\\,800.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186563759 1074 "From part 5, the required number of wooden tables $x$ is the solution to the equation\n\n$$\n6\\cdot ({x}^{2}-60x+800)=0.\n$$\n\nDividing this equation by $6,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-60x+800=0.\n$$\n\nTo solve this equation, we want to find a factor pair of the constant term $(800)$ that also adds up to the coefficient of the linear term $(-60).$ The required factors are $(-20)$ and $(-40).$\n\nUsing this factor pair, we write the linear term as $-60x=-20x-40x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-60x+800 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the first two terms and the last two terms separately and then factor the expression completely:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-20x-40x+800 & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-20x)+(-40x+800) & =0 \\\\ x(x-20)-40(x-20) & =0 \\\\ (x-20)(x-40)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the solutions are $x=20$ or $x=40.$ Since we require the minimum solution, we conclude that the company needs to produce $20$ wooden tables per week to make a profit of $\\$4\\,800.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186564501 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    $\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n    {{field-2}}\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n    $-2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186564501 1071 "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{8}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]x(t)+[\\begin{aligned}-6{t}^{2}+\\frac{41}{3}t-8 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}-\\frac{13}{3}t+5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,{t}^{2}+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,t+\\,\\,$\n  </td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n    .\n  </td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n$-2$\n  </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186564526 1071 "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 40%=32,$ then $\\,\\boxed{}=$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186564526 1071 "If $\\,\\boxed{}\\times 40\\%=32,$ then $\\,\\boxed{}=$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186564526 1074 "The part is $32,$ the percentage is $40%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{100}=\\frac{40÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =32÷40% \\\\  & =32÷\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =32\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2}\\times 5 \\\\  & =16\\times 5 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186564526 1074 "The part is $32,$ the percentage is $40\\%,$ and we need to find the whole.\n\nTo do this, we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{whole}=\\text{part}÷\\text{percentage}\n$$\n\nBefore we can substitute our numbers into the formula, we need to express $40\\%$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{40}{100}=\\frac{40÷20}{100÷20}=\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can now substitute our numbers into the formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{whole} & =32÷40\\% \\\\  & =32÷\\frac{2}{5} \\\\  & =32\\times \\frac{5}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{32}{2}\\times 5 \\\\  & =16\\times 5 \\\\  & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186566251 1071 "Nathan invested $\\$5\\,600$ into a company which then went on to lose $7%$ of its value every year. Which expression gives the value of Nathan's investment after $3$ years?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186566251 1071 "Nathan invested $\\$5\\,600$ into a company which then went on to lose $7\\%$ of its value every year. Which expression gives the value of Nathan's investment after $3$ years?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186566251 1074 "The number $7%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.07.$ Therefore, a decrease of $7%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.07=0.93\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)^{3}$ dollars." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186566251 1074 "The number $7\\%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.07.$ Therefore, a decrease of $7\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.07=0.93\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the value of the investment was $5\\,600\\cdot (0.93)^{3}$ dollars." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186579527 1071 "At the start of a study, a hive had $2\\,000$ bees. The study shows that the number of bees in the hive is expected to increase by $20%$ every month. What equation should be solved to determine the time that it will take for there to be $5\\,000$ bees in the hive?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186579527 1071 "At the start of a study, a hive had $2\\,000$ bees. The study shows that the number of bees in the hive is expected to increase by $20\\%$ every month. What equation should be solved to determine the time that it will take for there to be $5\\,000$ bees in the hive?" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186579527 1074 "The number of bees $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $20%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of bees.\n\n- $r=0.2$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the start of the study\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =2\\,000\\cdot (1+0.2)^{t} \\\\  & =2\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it takes  for the hive to increase to $5\\,000$  bees, we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\,000 \\\\ 2\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t} & =5\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186579527 1074 "The number of bees $y(t)$ grows exponentially with a growth rate of $20\\%.$\n\nThe formula for exponential growth (in terms of a growth rate $r$) is\n\n$$\ny=a(1+r)^{t}.\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have the following:\n\n- $a=2\\,000$ is the initial number of bees.\n\n- $r=0.2$ is the growth rate.\n\n- $t$ is the time, in months, since the start of the study\n\nSo, the expression for $y(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =2\\,000\\cdot (1+0.2)^{t} \\\\  & =2\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to find the time it takes  for the hive to increase to $5\\,000$  bees, we would need to solve the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(t) & =5\\,000 \\\\ 2\\,000\\cdot (1.2)^{t} & =5\\,000.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186579528 1071 "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of a student getting $9$ correct answers on a test with $12$ questions if each question has $3$ options and the student answers all questions randomly.\n\nII. The probability of finding more than $50$ people with blue eyes in a randomly selected sample of $250$ if $40%$ of the population has blue eyes.\n\nIII. The probability that a biased coin lands on tails $35$ times out of $50$ coin flips if the probability that a single flip landing on tails is $0.6.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186579528 1071 "Which of the following probabilities could be approximated using the normal approximation of the binomial distribution?\n\nI. The probability of a student getting $9$ correct answers on a test with $12$ questions if each question has $3$ options and the student answers all questions randomly.\n\nII. The probability of finding more than $50$ people with blue eyes in a randomly selected sample of $250$ if $40\\%$ of the population has blue eyes.\n\nIII. The probability that a biased coin lands on tails $35$ times out of $50$ coin flips if the probability that a single flip landing on tails is $0.6.$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186579532 1071 "In a city, $55%$ of the people have blood type O+. Using the normal approximation to the binomial distribution, approximate the probability that if $300$ people are picked at random, then $180$ of them will have blood type O+. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\