0$ will arrive early. Give your answer to three decimal places.\n\n*Hint: The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2420\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2389\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2358\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2743\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2709\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.2676\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.5\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3085\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3050\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3015\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,2.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9985\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9986\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9986\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,3.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9989\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9990\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9990\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,3.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9992\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9993\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9993\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583016 1071 "It is known that $24%$ of a given population have blood type A+. What is the probability that $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type A+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583016 1071 "It is known that $24\\%$ of a given population have blood type A+. What is the probability that $3$ of $10$ randomly selected people have blood type A+? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583016 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people having blood type A+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ people\n\n- the probability of having blood type A+ is $p=24%=0.24$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.24).$ We want to find the probability of having $x=3$ people with blood type A+.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{3})(0.24)^{3}(1-0.24)^{10-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{10!}{3!(10-3)!}(0.24)^{3}(0.76)^{7} \\\\  & =120(0.24)^{3}(0.76)^{7} \\\\  & =0.243\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583016 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of people having blood type A+.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ people\n\n- the probability of having blood type A+ is $p=24\\%=0.24$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, w