, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583017 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of seeds that will germinate.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=5$ seeds\n\n- the probability of the germination of the seeds is $p=75\\%=0.75$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.75).$ We want to find the probability that exactly $3$ seeds will germinate.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{3})(0.75)^{3}(1-0.75)^{5-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{5!}{3!(5-3)!}(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & =10(0.75)^{3}(0.25)^{2} \\\\  & ≈0.264\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583033 1071 "A student is randomly answering questions in a $10$-question math test. It is known that the student has a $25%$ probability of correctly answering each question. What is the probability that the student answers at most $1$ question correctly? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583033 1071 "A student is randomly answering questions in a $10$-question math test. It is known that the student has a $25\\%$ probability of correctly answering each question. What is the probability that the student answers at most $1$ question correctly? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583033 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of questions that the student answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ questions\n\n- the probability of answering each question correctly is $p=25%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.25).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the student answers correctly at most $1$ question. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{10-1} \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+10(0.25)(0.75)^{9} \\\\  & =0.244\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583033 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of questions that the student answers correctly.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=10$ questions\n\n- the probability of answering each question correctly is $p=25\\%=0.25$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.25).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the student answers correctly at most $1$ question. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 1) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1) \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{10-1} \\\\  & =(0.75)^{10}+10(0.25)(0.75)^{9} \\\\  & =0.244\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583041 1071 "An unfair coin has a $20%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583041 1071 "An unfair coin has a $20\\%$ chance of landing on heads. The coin is tossed $10$ times. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583041 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=20%=0.2.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{10-1} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\,(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2})(0.2)^{2}(0.8)^{10-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{10}+10(0.2)(0.8)^{9}+45(0.2)^{2}(0.8)^{8}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.677\\,800 \\\\  & ≈0.322,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583041 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of times the coin landed on heads.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=10$ tosses.\n\n- The probability of landing on heads is $p=20\\%=0.2.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(10,0.2).$ \n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(1-0.2)^{10-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{x})(0.2)^{x}(0.8)^{10-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of getting heads at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=10)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{0})(0.2)^{0}(0.8)^{10-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{1})(0.2)^{1}(0.8)^{10-1} \\\\  & \\,\\,+\\,(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{2})(0.2)^{2}(0.8)^{10-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.8)^{10}+10(0.2)(0.8)^{9}+45(0.2)^{2}(0.8)^{8}] \\\\  & ≈1-0.677\\,800 \\\\  & ≈0.322,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583042 1071 "A factory machine produces defective parts with probability $30%.$ If the machine produces $6$ parts, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that exactly $2$ or fewer of them are defective?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186583042 1071 "A factory machine produces defective parts with probability $30\\%.$ If the machine produces $6$ parts, rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that exactly $2$ or fewer of them are defective?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186583042 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of defective parts produced.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ parts.\n\n- The probability of a defective part is $p=30%=0.3.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.3).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the given values into the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.3)^{x}(1-0.3)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.3)^{x}(0.7)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that at most $2$ of the $6$ parts are defective. So, we compute\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 2) & =P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{0})(0.3)^{0}(0.7)^{6}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{1})(0.3)^{1}(0.7)^{5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{2})(0.3)^{2}(0.7)^{4} \\\\  & =1(0.7)^