n{bmatrix}2x-4y+5z \\\\ 6x+3y-2z \\\\ -8x-y+4z\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nHence, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 3 & -2 \\\\ -8 & -1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186593280 1071 "If\n    $Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${Z}^{T}=$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186593280 1071 "If\n$Z=\\begin{bmatrix}-1\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${Z}^{T}=$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186593290 1071 "A soccer player has a $70%$ chance of scoring a goal on a penalty kick. She takes $5$ penalty shots in a row. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that she scores at most $3$ goals?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186593290 1071 "A soccer player has a $70\\%$ chance of scoring a goal on a penalty kick. She takes $5$ penalty shots in a row. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that she scores at most $3$ goals?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186593290 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of goals scored.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ penalty shots.\n\n- The probability of scoring is $p=70%=0.7.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.7).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that she scores at most $3$ goals. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 3)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=3),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=4)+P(X=5)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.7)^{4}(0.3)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.7)^{5}(0.3)^{5-5}] \\\\  & =1-[5(0.7)^{4}(0.3)+(0.7)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.528\\,220 \\\\  & ≈0.472,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186593290 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of goals scored.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=5$ penalty shots.\n\n- The probability of scoring is $p=70\\%=0.7.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(5,0.7).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(1-0.7)^{5-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{x})(0.7)^{x}(0.3)^{5-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that she scores at most $3$ goals. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 3)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=3),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 3) & =1-P(X>3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=4)+P(X=5)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{4})(0.7)^{4}(0.3)^{5-4}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{5}{5})(0.7)^{5}(0.3)^{5-5}] \\\\  & =1-[5(0.7)^{4}(0.3)+(0.7)^{5}] \\\\  & =1-0.528\\,220 \\\\  & ≈0.472,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186593303 1071 "If\n    $N=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 2 & -15\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${N}^{T}=$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186593303 1071 "If\n$N=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & 2 & -15\\end{bmatrix},$ then ${N}^{T}=$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186597612 1071 "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, species $1$, species $2$, species $3,$ and species $4.$ Each species has the same population.\n\n- $8%$ of squirrels of species $1$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $9%$ of squirrels of species $2$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $11%$ of squirrels of species $4$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $42%$ of the squirrels are adult females, $8%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, and $36%$ of the squirrels born in captivity are adult females, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel from species $3$ is born in captivity? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186597612 1071 "In a nature park, there are $4$ species of squirrels, species $1$, species $2$, species $3,$ and species $4.$ Each species has the same population.\n\n- $8\\%$ of squirrels of species $1$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $9\\%$ of squirrels of species $2$ are born in captivity.\n\n- $11\\%$ of squirrels of species $4$ are born in captivity.\n\nIf $42\\%$ of the squirrels are adult females, $8\\%$ of the adult female squirrels are born in captivity, and $36\\%$ of the squirrels born in captivity are adult females, what is the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel from species $3$ is born in captivity? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186597612 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{S}_{3},{S}_{4}$ be the event that a squirrel is of species $1,$ $2,$ $3,$ or $4,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- Each species has the same number of individuals, so we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({S}_{1}) &=P({S}_{2})=P({S}_{3})=P({S}_{4})=0.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.08 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.09 \\\\  & P(C|{S}_{4})=0.11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}⊔{S}_{3}⊔{S}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})+P({S}_{3})+P({S}_{4})=0.25+0.25+0.25+0.25=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $42%$ of the squirrels are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.42.\n$$\n\n- Since $8%$ of adult females are born in captivity, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.08.\n$$\n\n- Since $36%$ of squirrels born in captivity are adult females, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|C)=0.36.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen squirrel from species $3$ is born in captivity, which is represented as $P(C|{S}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})+P(C|{S}_{3})P({S}_{3})+P(C|{S}_{4})P({S}_{4})} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{(0.08)(0.42)}{(0.08)(0.25)+(0.09)(0.25)+P(C|{S}_{3})(0.25)+(0.11)(0.25)} \\\\ 0.36 & =\\frac{0.0336}{0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C|{S}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.36(0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3})) & =0.0336 \\\\ 0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.0336}{0.36} \\\\ 0.07+0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{75} \\\\ 0.25P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{7}{75}-0.07 \\\\ P(C|{S}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.25}(\\frac{7}{75}-0.07) \\\\ P(C|{S}_{3}) & ≈0.093\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186597612 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a squirrel is an adult female, $C$ be the event that a squirrel is born in captivity, and ${S}_{1},{S}_{2},{