071 "The average waiting time in a bank is $16\\,\\text{min}.$ Additionally, $25\\%$ of the customers are assisted in less than $11$ minutes. Assuming that the customer waiting times are normally distributed, find the percentage of customers that wait $20$ minutes or more, rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6664\\,$ | $\\,0.6700\\,$ | $\\,0.6736\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.7019\\,$ | $\\,0.7054\\,$ | $\\,0.7088\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186606836 1074 "Let $X$ represent a customer's waiting time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(16,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\ge 20).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<11)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11-16}{\\sigma }) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(-\\frac{5}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)≈-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =-0.6745 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1349 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.1349} \\\\  & ≈7.4129.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\ge 20)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 20) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{20-16}{7.4129}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 0.54) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.54) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.54) \\\\  & =1-0.7054 \\\\  & ≈29.5%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $29.5%$ of the customers wait $20$ minutes or more." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186606836 1074 "Let $X$ represent a customer's waiting time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(16,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X\\ge 20).$ But first, we need to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<11)=0.25.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<11) & =0.25 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{11-16}{\\sigma }) & =0.25 \\\\ Φ\\left(-\\frac{5}{\\sigma }\\right) & =0.25 \\\\ -\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.25)≈-0.6745.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{\\sigma } & =-0.6745 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1349 \\\\ \\sigma  & =\\frac{1}{0.1349} \\\\  & ≈7.4129.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X\\ge 20)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 20) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{20-16}{7.4129}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\ge 0.54) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.54) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.54) \\\\  & =1-0.7054 \\\\  & ≈29.5\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $29.5\\%$ of the customers wait $20$ minutes or more." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186606840 1071 "Which model shows $56%?$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186606840 1071 "Which model shows $56\\%?$" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186606840 1074 "Recall that $56%$ is the same as $56$ out of $100.$ \n\nSo, we need a model that shows $56$ shaded tiles out of $100.$ The correct model is:\n\n![](images/4c/4c50961e1acc7e230994f0b0fee6c25b8490e5f15b8d49347b6fe2dd367b3a28.png)" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186606840 1074 "Recall that $56\\%$ is the same as $56$ out of $100.$ \n\nSo, we need a mode