,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ | $\\,0.2877\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ | $\\,0.3228\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ |" 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186606860 1071 "The duration of canine pregnancies is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu$ days and a standard deviation of $12$ days. Additionally, $10\\%$ of the pregnancies last less than $250$ days. Find the percentage of pregnancies with a duration greater than $260$ days.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.2946\\,$ | $\\,0.2912\\,$ | $\\,0.2877\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.3300\\,$ | $\\,0.3264\\,$ | $\\,0.3228\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.3669\\,$ | $\\,0.3632\\,$ | $\\,0.3594\\,$ |" 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186606860 1074 "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected pregnancy. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{12}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>260).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<250)=0.10.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<250) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{250-\\mu }{12}) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{250-\\mu }{12}\\right) & =0.10 \\\\ \\frac{250-\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)≈-1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{250-\\mu }{12} & =-1.2816 \\\\ 250-\\mu  & =-15.3792 \\\\ \\mu  & =265.3792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(260<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>260) & =P(Z>\\frac{260-265.3792}{12}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.45) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.45) \\\\  & =1-0.3264 \\\\  & ≈67.4%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $67.4%$ of the pregnancies have a duration greater than $260$ days." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186606860 1074 "Let $X$ represent the length of a randomly selected pregnancy. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{12}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X>260).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X<250)=0.10.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<250) & =0.10 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{250-\\mu }{12}) & =0.10 \\\\ Φ\\left(\\frac{250-\\mu }{12}\\right) & =0.10 \\\\ \\frac{250-\\mu }{12} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.10)≈-1.2816.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{250-\\mu }{12} & =-1.2816 \\\\ 250-\\mu  & =-15.3792 \\\\ \\mu  & =265.3792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(260<X)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>260) & =P(Z>\\frac{260-265.3792}{12}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>-0.45) \\\\  & =1-Φ(-0.45) \\\\  & =1-0.3264 \\\\  & ≈67.4\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $67.4\\%$ of the pregnancies have a duration greater than $260$ days." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186606861 1074 "One large square representing one whole is divided into $100$ equal tile