 17592186576411 true] [17592186618740 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=35%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(15,0.35).$ We want to find the probability that $x=7$ customers order their coffee to go.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=7) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{7})(0.35)^{7}(1-0.35)^{15-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{15!}{7!(15-7)!}(0.35)^{7}(0.65)^{8} \\\\  & =6\\,435(0.35)^{7}(0.65)^{8} \\\\  & =0.132\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186618740 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of customers that order their coffee to go.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- there are $n=15$ customers\n\n- the probability that each customer orders the coffee to go is $p=35\\%=0.35$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(15,0.35).$ We want to find the probability that $x=7$ customers order their coffee to go.\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=7) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{15}{7})(0.35)^{7}(1-0.35)^{15-7} \\\\  & =\\frac{15!}{7!(15-7)!}(0.35)^{7}(0.65)^{8} \\\\  & =6\\,435(0.35)^{7}(0.65)^{8} \\\\  & =0.132\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186618743 1071 "A basketball player has a $25%$ chance of making a half-court shot. She attempts the shot $8$ times during practice. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that she makes the shot at least $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186618743 1071 "A basketball player has a $25\\%$ chance of making a half-court shot. She attempts the shot $8$ times during practice. Rounded to $3$ decimal places, what is the probability that she makes the shot at least $3$ times?\n\nRounded to $3$ decimal places, the probability is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186618743 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful half-court shots.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=8$ attempts.\n\n- The probability of making a shot is $p=25%=0.25.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.25).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of making the shot at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0})(0.25)^{0}(0.75)^{8-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{8-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2})(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{8-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.75)^{8}+8(0.25)(0.75)^{7}+28(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{6}] \\\\  & =1-0.678\\,543… \\\\  & ≈0.321,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186618743 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful half-court shots.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=8$ attempts.\n\n- The probability of making a shot is $p=25\\%=0.25.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(8,0.25).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.25)^{x}(1-0.25)^{8-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{x})(0.25)^{x}(0.75)^{8-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability of making the shot at least $3$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\ge 3).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\ge 3)=P(X=3)+P(X=4)+…+P(X=8)\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 3) & =1-P(X<3) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=0)+P(X=1)+P(X=2)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{0})(0.25)^{0}(0.75)^{8-0}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{1})(0.25)^{1}(0.75)^{8-1}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{8}{2})(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{8-2}] \\\\  & =1-[(0.75)^{8}+8(0.25)(0.75)^{7}+28(0.25)^{2}(0.75)^{6}] \\\\  & =1-0.678\\,543… \\\\  & ≈0.321,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 true] [17592186618765 1071 "A language school has $160$ students enrolled. Of t