ction, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.75)^{x}(1-0.75)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.75)^{x}(0.25)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the machine completes the task correctly at most $4$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 4).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 4)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=4),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 4) & =1-P(X>4) \\\\  & =1-[P(X=5)+P(X=6)] \\\\  & =1-[(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{5})(0.75)^{5}(0.25)^{6-5}+(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{6})(0.75)^{6}(0.25)^{6-6}] \\\\  & =1-[6(0.75)^{5}(0.25)+(0.75)^{6}] \\\\  & =1-0.533\\,935… \\\\  & ≈0.466,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139539471 17592186576411 false] [17592186618787 1074 "Let the random variable $X$ be equal to the number of successful completions.\n\nHere, we have the following information:\n\n- There are $n=6$ tasks.\n\n- The probability of success is $p=75\\%=0.75.$\n\nSo, $X$ follows a binomial distribution, where $X∼B(6,0.75).$\n\nThe number of successes of a binomial random variable has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nP(X=x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){p}^{x}(1-p)^{n-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of experiments, and $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment.\n\nSubstituting the above information into the formula for the probability mass function, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=x) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.75)^{x}(1-0.75)^{6-x} \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x})(0.75)^{x}(0.25)^{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that the machine completes the task correctly at most $4$ times. This corresponds to $P(X\\le 4).$\n\nHowever, computing\n\n$$\nP(X\\le 4)=P(X=0)+P(X=1)+…+P(X=4),\n$$\n\nwill involve many computations, so let's express our answer in terms of the complement instead. By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n